            alt: 'A round seal. A gold ring encloses a sky-blue field, on which lies a white rose of five rounded petals, one pointing upwards. In the middle of the rose is a large upright red heart, and inside the heart a black Latin cross.',
            about: 'Luther’s books bore a rose before he explained it: in 1524 a rose device with his initials M L closed the second part of his Old Testament, as witness that the book had passed through his hands. The explanation came on 8 July 1530, from the Coburg, when Lazarus Spengler asked whether the seal was right. He took it as the mark of his theology and glossed every part: a black cross, for faith in the Crucified; a heart left its natural colour, because the cross mortifies but does not kill; a white rose, not red, for the joy and peace of faith; a sky-blue field, for the heavenly joy to come; a gold ring, because that bliss has no end.',
            drawn: 'Follows the letter’s five elements and tinctures: a black Latin cross in an upright red heart, the heart in the middle of a white rose of five petals, on a sky-blue field within a gold ring. Shapes, proportions and the petal set (one uppermost) are conventional, not traced from an original. Shading is modelling only. The letter names nothing more, so nothing more is drawn: not the initials M L that accompany the rose on the 1524 device, not the sepals of the emblem’s usual form, whose colour no source here gives, and no later motto.',
            source: 'Martin Luther, letter to Lazarus Spengler, Veste Coburg, 8 July 1530 (WA Br 5, no. 1628), written in answer to Spengler’s question whether Luther’s seal (“Petschaft”) had been rightly made. Related originals, named here but not inspected for this drawing: the gold signet ring with Luther’s arms cut in carnelian, now in the Grünes Gewölbe, Dresden, a gift of Prince John Frederick of Saxony, which a grandson of Luther gave to Elector John George I, as J. L. Sponsel records in *Das Grüne Gewölbe zu Dresden*, vol. 3 (1929); and the rose device with the initials M L, printed with an Agnus Dei at the end of *Das Ander teyl des alten testaments* (Wittenberg: Christian Döring and Lucas Cranach, 1524) as a mark of Luther’s authorised editions.',
