            alt: 'A slender gold sign on a dark ground. A thin crescent moon, horns up, cuts across the top of a large ring with a dot at its centre. Below the ring hangs a cross as wide as the ring, with a short upper arm and a long lower stem. The stem ends between two small arches, which rise from its foot and come down on either side like a pair of horns.',
            about: 'John Dee’s *Monas hieroglyphica*, printed at Antwerp by Willem Silvius in 1564 and addressed to Maximilian, King of the Romans, takes twenty-four theorems to build one sign out of the point, the line and the circle. The Moon’s horns stand over the Sun, and the point at the Sun’s centre is the Earth. Below them hang the cross of the four elements and, at the foot, the sign of Aries, for fire. Dee prescribed even the thickness of its lines. Fifty-two years later it was printed beside the invitation in the *Chymical Wedding*, whose text never says a word about it.',
            drawn: 'Follows Dee’s construction in Theorem XXIII, with his line AB as the unit. The cross is two equal lines of four units. The bar is halved, and the upright is cut one unit above the bar and three below. The Sun and the Moon share one radius of two units, and the Moon’s centre stands one unit above the top of the Sun, so the crescent runs across the Sun’s upper part. Aries is two semicircles of one unit, joined at the foot of the cross. Every line has the body Dee prescribes, a quarter of AB. The bar’s length is worked out from his nine-part height. The woodcut was not seen, so two points are inferred: that Aries stands on its base line like two arches, and that the Sun’s ring lies just inside its circle. The lettering is omitted, and the printed black becomes gold.',
            source: 'John Dee, *Monas hieroglyphica* (Antwerp: Willem Silvius, 1564), addressed to Maximilian, King of the Romans, of Bohemia and of Hungary. The sign follows Dee’s construction in the theorems, with its proportions, the weight of its lines and the shape of its crescent taken from Theorem XXIII, as translated by J. W. Hamilton-Jones (1947). It was printed again beside the text of the invitation in the *Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz* (Strasbourg: heirs of Lazarus Zetzner, 1616), p. 5. That page number is the one cited in ‘The Hermetic Frontispiece’, *Ambix*, 2017.',
