            about: 'The title vignette of *Summum Bonum* (1629), a defence of Fludd and the Rosicrucians against the Minim friar Marin Mersenne, printed under the name Joachim Frizius. It has long been laid at Fludd’s door, and he did take the emblem into his own *Clavis*. The motto says what the picture shows: the rose gives honey to the bees. The rose has seven circles of seven petals and grows on a thorny, cross-shaped stem, so the Rosy Cross becomes a plant. Four hives stand on the right. On the left, two spiders’ webs, each also divided in seven, hang on a grape trellis. Joscelyn Godwin suggested that the spiders stand for lunar forces and the bees for solar ones, with the rose reconciling the two.',
