            alt: 'A Masonic jewel drawn in gold on dark. A crown sits on the hinge of a pair of compasses whose narrow-set legs rest their points on a short curved arc. Between the legs stands a red Latin cross with a gold rose at its crossing, the rose’s stem winding round the lower part of the cross. At the foot a pelican bends to pierce its breast over a nest of young.',
            about: 'The Rose-Croix degree appeared in French Freemasonry in the 1760s and became the eighteenth degree of the Ancient and Accepted Scottish Rite, whose first Supreme Council met at Charleston in 1801. Its name is the French name for the Rosicrucians, in print in France by 1623, when Naudé took the affair apart. Its matter is not the Fama’s: there is no Christian Rosenkreuz, no seven-sided vault and nothing of the Fama’s story. It turns on the Passion, and its word is I.N.R.I. A London Masonic magazine described its jewel in 1858: a crowned pair of compasses opened on an arc of a sixteenth of a circle, and between the legs a passion cross ‘formed of rubies’ with a rose at its centre. The rose’s stem twines round the lower limb, and at the foot a pelican wounds her breast to feed her young. An eagle takes the pelican’s place on the reverse. The pelican feeding her young with her blood is a Christian sign of Christ’s sacrifice, and the manifestos do not use it.',
            drawn: 'Follows the description of the jewel printed in a Masonic magazine of 1 June 1858: crowned compasses opened on an arc of a sixteenth of a circle, a passion cross ‘formed of rubies’ between the legs with a full-blown rose at its centre and its stem twined round the lower limb, and a pelican feeding her young at the foot. Only the obverse is drawn. The crown’s form, the leaves, the number of young and every proportion except the angle are the drawing’s own. The cipher on the arc is left out because its letters are not established. The cross is red, for the rubies of the 1858 text, and is shown as a row of square stones, which is the drawing’s own; everything else is gold on dark.',
            source: 'Description of the jewel of the Rose Croix degree in the London Freemasons’ Magazine series (masonicperiodicals.org, series ‘mmr’, issue of 1 June 1858, p. 195; the article’s title and the masthead of that issue not verified). A later description in the same series, 3 July 1869, p. 12, gives the compasses at 60°.',
