    brethrenSuccession: [
        'C.R., a German of noble but poor parents, is put in a cloister in his fifth year. As a youth he is ‘associated’ to Brother P.A.L. for the Holy Land, and P.A.L. dies in Cyprus. The journey is on the map.',
        'Back in Germany he builds a house. After five years he calls three brethren from his first cloister, G.V., J.A. and J.O. He binds them to be faithful, diligent and secret, and to write down all he teaches. ‘After this manner began the Fraternity of the Rosie Cross; first, by four persons onely’.',
        'The four make the magical language and writing, with a large dictionary, and the first part of the Book M.',
        'The work grows too heavy, the sick are too many, and the house Sancti spiritus is finished. Four more are chosen: R.C., his cousin; B., a painter; G. (G.G. in the signatures); and P.D., their secretary. All are German except J.A. They are eight, all bachelors and vowed to virginity. Together they collect a book ‘of all that which man can desire, wish, or hope for’.',
        'Their teaching complete, they part for several countries under six articles, which are quoted above.',
        'Five leave at once. B. and D. stay a year with the founder. When they too leave, the cousin and J.O. remain by him, so that, the text says, he has two brothers with him all his life. Every year they meet.',
        'The first to die is J.O., in England. Burial places are kept secret and some are lost, but each place is filled by a successor.',
        'After J.O.’s death the founder calls the rest together. The narrators suppose that it was then his grave was made.',
        'The founder dies at more than a hundred, among his brothers, on a date the narrators do not know. The Confessio supplies one: born in 1378, with a life of 106 years. Eight entries are signed under his epitaph: five of the first circle and, in a second circle, P.A. for I.O., A. for P.D. and R. for the founder. I.O. and D. are already dead.',
        'The narrators know little of the second row and are not certain that it had the first row’s wisdom. One of its members, A., successor to D., lives among them, the third row, and in dark words passes on a secret concerning the hundred years.',
        'A. dies in Gallia Narbonensis. N.N. succeeds him and takes the oath. He reports A.’s promise that the Fraternity would soon serve the whole German nation.',
        'The next year N.N., an architect, alters the building and finds the brass table of names. In moving it he uncovers the door: ‘Post 120 annos patebo’. The vault is drawn above.',
        'In the vault the narrators find the founder’s Itinerarium and life, ‘whence this relation for the most part is taken’. They also find the body, the book in his hand (‘called I.’ in the English of 1652), the epitaph and the signatures.',
        'They reseal the vault, part, and leave ‘the natural heirs in possession of our Jewels’. They publish the brothers’ initials so that readers will search for the graves. They hope that ‘some few, which shall give their names, may joyn together, thereby to increase the number’.',
    ],
    brethrenCoda: 'The line is built to be checked. The brothers sign under the founder’s epitaph, and a brass table ‘containeth all the names of the brethren’. The narrators print the initials in the hope ‘that this our Example will stir up others more deligently to enquire after their names (whom we have therefore published) and to search for the place of their burial’. Most of them, they add, ‘are yet known, and praised among very old folks’. No grave or table has ever been found, and no name recovered. The one historical figure the Fama sets beside the line stands outside it: Theophrastus Paracelsus. He ‘was none of our Fraternity’, yet ‘diligently read over the Book M’, and his ‘Vocabular’ lay in the vault. The English in which the line is quoted here is not Vaughan’s own. ‘The Translation of the Fama belongs to an unknown hand’, he wrote in ‘The Publisher to the Reader’. He named one of its mistakes, Damascus for Damcar, and would not alter it, ‘for I am no Pedant to correct another mans Labours’. In his Preface he added that he had ‘no acquaintance with this Fraternity as to their Persons’. The line is evidence of how the Fama was made and read. It is not evidence that any of the brothers lived.',

