    circlesCoda: 'Read from top to bottom, the two halves show the same thing from opposite ends. Before 1700 the people can be found and the brotherhood cannot: every name here stands on a title page or in a letter, and no one could show that he had reached the brothers. After 1710 the brotherhoods are real, with rules, grades, offices and charters, and their ties to the first circles run through print. What passed from Tübingen by documented hands was Andreae’s plan for a Christian society, to Comenius and the Hartlib circle. The charters of the later orders pass between living men, from Westcott to Reuss and from Reuss to Steiner, Crowley and Lewis, and none of them reaches back to Tübingen except by assertion.',

